body:
Theme CSS library references: a theme's `theme.css` — and every CSS file it reaches through `@import`, wherever in the library that file lives — may reference any servable file in the resolved template library with both `url(...)` and `@import`. Themes are selectable, not sealed off: several themes share most of their CSS (slide structure, spacing, typography, `@font-face` rules) and their brand files (fonts, logos, backgrounds), so files are written once instead of being copied into every `themes/<name>/` and drifting apart.

Two reserved URL prefixes extend the reference vocabulary. Both are valid in theme CSS and in any CSS an `@import` pulls in, for `url()` and for `@import` alike:
- `media:path` — the existing reserved prefix (internal/template.MediaURLPrefix, the single source of truth): `path` is relative to the resolved library's shared `media/` tree and stays within it, e.g. `url('media:fonts/X.woff2')`, `@import url('media:css/slides.css')`. It never reaches a theme directory. `media:` is now accepted on `@import` as well as on `url()`.
- `theme:<name>/path` — the new cross-theme prefix: `<name>` names a sibling directory under the resolved library's `themes/`, and `path` is relative to that theme's directory and stays within it, e.g. `@import url('theme:default/theme.css')`, `url('theme:default/fonts/X.woff2')`. An unknown `<name>` is a templates error naming the CSS file and line. It never reaches outside the library.

A reference with no reserved prefix is a relative path resolved from the referencing CSS file's own location — its own directory, whether that is a theme directory or a subtree of `media/` — exactly as a browser resolves a relative CSS URL; it must stay inside the library root.

Imported CSS gets the same treatment as theme CSS: its relative references resolve from its own location, and every reference in it is checked at load time (templates-dir-validation step 6) and rewritten at serve time. A stylesheet imported through `media:` is served and rewritten from the `media/` route; one imported through `theme:<name>/` is served and rewritten from that theme's route.

Guards retained: no absolute paths; no `..` escaping the containing tree (a `media:` path stays within `media/`, a `theme:<name>/` path stays within that theme, a bare relative reference stays inside the library root); no reaching outside the library (no `http:`, `https:`, `data:`, `//` or any other scheme); and `@import` is bounded and cycle-safe, with cycles detected across the whole import graph (same-theme, theme↔media, theme↔theme). A missing file, a bad reference or a cycle is a templates error naming the CSS file and line (requirements.constraint.media-paths-library-relative). At serve time kalide rewrites every reserved-prefix reference — in the served theme CSS and in every served stylesheet it imports — to the served URL (the media URL the layout `media` helper returns, or that theme's served file URL), so the browser fetches the file from the resolved library. Both embedded authoring guides document the syntax (agent-authoring, library-agent-guide, agent-guide-drift-check).

AC:
- theme CSS can `url()` and `@import` a file under the resolved library's `media/` (`media:path`) and a file in another theme (`theme:<name>/path`); the served CSS exposes the served URL, which resolves in the rendered deck (one shared copy for every theme that references it);
- imported CSS is itself fully checked and rewritten: a `media:`/`theme:` reference or a relative reference inside an imported stylesheet (whether the import target is in the same theme, another theme, or `media/`) is validated at load and rewritten at serve exactly like a reference in `theme.css`;
- a `media:` reference/import to a missing file, an absolute path or a `..` escaping `media/`, and a `theme:<name>/` reference/import to an unknown theme, a missing file, an absolute path or a `..` escaping that theme, are each a templates error naming the CSS file and line;
- an `@import` cycle — including one spanning a theme and `media/` or two themes — is a templates error naming the CSS file and line;
- both embedded guides document both prefixes, drop the rule that a theme reaches only its own directory and the rule that `media:` is forbidden on `@import`, and removing the `media:` or the `theme:` prefix token from BOTH guides fails the agent-guide drift self-test (agent-guide-drift-check).
